Rendering of the proposed first lunar hotel. Image credit: GRU Space

The first lunar hotel is now open for reservations

With global travel numbers on the rise, sometimes it feels as though there =
are few places on Earth left untapped by swarms of tourists. For a fortunat=
e few of us, however, one off-the-beaten-path destination with out-of-this-=
world views may soon become an option.

California-based startup GRU Space is building a hotel on the moon =E2=80=
=94 and, as of this week, it=E2=80=99s officially taking reservations for i=
ts grand opening, slated for 2032.

The top qualification: being astronomically wealthy. Hopeful space guests m=
ust put down a deposit of $250k or $1m, depending on their selection, thoug=
h the total cost of the stay is expected (https://www.gru.space/reserve?_hs=
enc=3Dp2ANqtz-8bBK8XjwKMP4mja5t19DquOvu-KM5GmJMvTn_JoAiFR2R7d7RP4QIbSxC6X80=
_1GG8sD9MqOU4ZaNNbNvUg-_GkMi0Lg&_hsmi=3D398809432#form-reserve ) to exceed =
$10m.

The plan

Starting in 2029, the company will lead two lunar missions:

- Mission I will involve: launching a small payload, including an inflatabl=
e structure that will demonstrate the material intended to eventually be us=
ed in the hotel=E2=80=99s construction, and testing development of =E2=80=
=9Cmoon bricks=E2=80=9D using lunar regoliths (or, essentially, soil) and g=
eopolymers, per (https://arstechnica.com/space/2026/01/you-can-now-reserve-=
a-hotel-room-on-the-moon-for-250000/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8bBK8XjwKMP4mja5t19Dq=
uOvu-KM5GmJMvTn_JoAiFR2R7d7RP4QIbSxC6X80_1GG8sD9MqOU4ZaNNbNvUg-_GkMi0Lg&_hs=
mi=3D398809432 ) Ars Technica.

- For mission II, it=E2=80=99ll deploy a larger inflatable structure into a=
 =E2=80=9Clunar pit=E2=80=9D meant to shield it from extreme temperatures, =
and scale its resource development.

Come 2032, the initial version of the hotel, an inflatable structure built =
on Earth and deployed to the moon, will be able to accommodate up to four g=
uests as well as activities like moonwalking, golfing, and more.

Though it might not be as worth the money as the final iteration: a structu=
re modeled after San Francisco=E2=80=99s Palace of the Fine Arts and built =
from those aforementioned moon bricks.

Its ultimate goal: to help jumpstart the lunar economy and bring humanity t=
o =E2=80=9Cthe next frontier (https://www.gru.space/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8bBK8=
XjwKMP4mja5t19DquOvu-KM5GmJMvTn_JoAiFR2R7d7RP4QIbSxC6X80_1GG8sD9MqOU4ZaNNbN=
vUg-_GkMi0Lg&_hsmi=3D398809432 ) ,=E2=80=9D which will eventually include M=
ars.

Sound a bit far-fetched?

For sure =E2=80=94 especially when considering the one-year-old company=E2=
=80=99s team currently consists of just three people, including founder Sky=
ler Chan, who is just 22 years old =E2=80=94 but, to be fair, so did the id=
ea of sending people to space in 1969.

While there=E2=80=99s definitely room for skepticism, GRU has already recei=
ved backing from Y Combinator, Nvidia, and investors (https://gizmodo.com/w=
ant-to-book-a-hotel-on-the-moon-this-startup-is-now-taking-250k-deposits-20=
00709632?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8bBK8XjwKMP4mja5t19DquOvu-KM5GmJMvTn_JoAiFR2R7d7R=
P4QIbSxC6X80_1GG8sD9MqOU4ZaNNbNvUg-_GkMi0Lg&_hsmi=3D398809432 ) at Anduril =
and SpaceX, which has to count for something.

Plus, it=E2=80=99ll undergo YC=E2=80=99s three-month program this year to h=
elp the company develop its product and potentially raise additional fundin=
g.

In any case, we=E2=80=99ll just have to wait and see how things pan out. Bu=
t, as they say, shoot for the moon, right?

